L000147	\section{Details on the scalar theory} \label{app:theory}
L000148	
L000149	In the following, we provide further details on the nonlinear scalar theory outlined in the main text. To define the coupling coefficients $N^{\bar{m}mm'm''}_{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''}(\Psi)$ used in the main text, we being by introducing
L000150	\begin{align}
L000151	D^{\bar{m}mm'm''}_{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''} \ & =\int_{S^2} d\Omega \ Y^*_{\bar{\ell}\bar{m}}Y_{\ell m}Y^*_{\ell'' m''}Y_{\ell'm'}.
L000152	\end{align}
L000153	This appears, when projecting the scalar self-interaction $\Psi |\Psi|^2$ onto the spherical harmonic mode $(\bar{\ell},\bar{m})$ and can be integrated explicitly to
L000154	\begin{align}
L000155	\begin{aligned}
L000156	 & \ D^{\bar{m}mm'm''}_{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''} = (-1)^{m''+m}C_{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''} \\
L000157	& \ \qquad \times \sum_j\frac{2j+1}{4\pi}
L000158	\begin{pmatrix}
L000159	\bar{\ell} & \ell & j \\
L000160	0 & 0 & 0
L000161	\end{pmatrix}
L000162	\begin{pmatrix}
L000163	\ell' & \ell'' & j \\
L000164	0 & 0 & 0
L000165	\end{pmatrix}
L000166	\\
L000167	& \ \qquad \times
L000168	\begin{pmatrix}
L000169	\bar{\ell} & \ell & j \\
L000170	-\bar{m} & m & m'-m''
L000171	\end{pmatrix}
L000172	\begin{pmatrix}
L000173	\ell' & \ell'' & j \\
L000174	m' & -m'' & m-\bar{m}
L000175	\end{pmatrix},
L000176	\end{aligned}
L000177	\label{eq:Dllll}
L000178	\end{align}
L000179	using $C_{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''}=\sqrt{2\bar{\ell}+1}\sqrt{2\ell+1}\sqrt{2\ell'+1}\sqrt{2\ell''+1}$ and $(\cdot)$ denoting the Wigner 3-j symbol. For the derivative self-interactions, the coefficients
L000180	\begin{align}
L000181	E^{\bar{m}mm'm''}_{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''}=\int d\Omega \frac{Y^*_{\bar{\ell}\bar{m}}Y_{\ell m} Y_{\ell' m'}Y^*_{\ell'' m''}}{\sin^2\theta},
L000182	\label{eq:integralE}
L000183	\end{align}
L000184	are needed. These coefficients and $D^{\bar{m}mm'm''}_{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''}$ enter the nonlinear couplings
L000185	\begin{widetext}
L000186	\begin{align}
L000187	\begin{aligned}
L000188	N^{\bar{m}m m' m''}_{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''}(\Psi)=\ & \frac{\kappa f}{r^2}\phi_{\ell m}\phi_{\ell'm'}\phi^*_{\ell''m''} D^{\bar{m}m m' m''}_{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''}+\frac{\alpha f^2}{l r^4}\Bigg[D^{\bar{m}m m' m''}_{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''}\Big(\frac{l}{f^2}(m\Omega\phi_{\ell m}-ir\dot{\phi}_{\ell m})(m'\Omega\phi_{\ell' m'}-ir\dot{\phi}_{\ell' m'}) \\
L000189	& +(\phi_{\ell m}-r\partial_r\phi_{\ell m})(\phi_{\ell' m'}-r\partial_r\phi_{\ell' m'}) \Big)\phi^*_{\ell'' m''}+\phi_{\ell m}\phi_{\ell' m'}\phi^*_{\ell'' m''}\Big( -mm'E^{\bar{m}m m' m''}_{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''} \\
L000190	& + \tilde{c}_{++}E^{\bar{m}m m' m''}_{\bar{\ell},\ell+1,\ell'+1,\ell''}+\tilde{c}_{+-}E^{\bar{m}m m' m''}_{\bar{\ell},\ell+1,\ell'-1,\ell''}+\tilde{c}_{-+}E^{\bar{m}m m' m''}_{\bar{\ell},\ell-1,\ell'+1,\ell''}+\tilde{c}_{--}E^{\bar{m}m m' m''}_{\bar{\ell},\ell-1,\ell'-1,\ell''} \Big)\Bigg].
L000191	\end{aligned}
L000192	\label{eq:N}
L000193	\end{align}
L000194	Here we used
L000195	\begin{align}
L000196	\tilde{c}_{++}= \ & \ell\ell'\left[\frac{(1+\ell-m)(1+\ell+m)(1+\ell'+m')(1+\ell'-m')}{(3+4\ell(2+\ell))(3+4\ell'(2+\ell'))}\right]^{1/2},\\
L000197	\tilde{c}_{-+}= \ & -\ell'(\ell+1)\left[\frac{(\ell+m)(1+\ell'+m')(1+\ell'-m')(\ell-m)}{(3+2\ell')(4\ell^2-1)(2\ell'+1)}\right]^{1/2},\\
L000198	\tilde{c}_{--}= \ & (\ell+1)(\ell'+1)\left[\frac{(\ell^2-m^2)(\ell'^2-m'^2)}{(4\ell'^2-1)(4\ell^2-1)}\right]^{1/2},
L000199	\end{align}
L000200	where $\tilde{c}_{+-}= \tilde{c}_{-+} \ (\ell \leftrightarrow \ell',m \leftrightarrow m')$. Note, for $\ell=\ell'=0$ all $\tilde{c}$ coefficients vanish.
L000201	\end{widetext}
L000202	
L000203	The integral \eqref{eq:integralE} is divergent for $\bar{m}=m=m'=m''=0$, independently of the polar mode numbers. This is irrelevant for the term $\sim mm'E^{\bar{m}m m' m''}_{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''}$, and when assuming $\bar{\ell},\ell,\ell',\ell''=0$, also for the last line in eq.~\eqref{eq:N}. While not manifestly finite, the divergences cancel exactly in the last line of eq.~\eqref{eq:N} also for $\bar{\ell},\ell,\ell',\ell''>0$. Regardless, all solutions of interest in this work are obtained neglecting the higher-order polar modes for $\bar{m}=m=m'=m''=0$, therefore, circumventing any formally divergent terms in the above expression for $N^{\bar{m}m m' m''}_{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''}(\Psi)$.
L000204	
L000205	Let us gain intuition for the coefficients $D^{\bar{m}mm'm''}_{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''}$ and $E^{\bar{m}mm'm''}_{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''}$. When focussing on the coupling between the fastest growing ergoregion unstable modes, i.e., $\ell=m$ etc., then these coefficients reduce to $D^{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''}_{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''}$ and $E^{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''}_{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''}$, which are non-vanishing if $\bar{\ell}+\ell''=\ell+\ell'$. This resonance condition corresponds to a plane in three-dimensional index-space for a given mode $\bar{\ell}$. As an example, in Fig.~\ref{fig:indexspace}, we show those non-vanishing coefficients sourcing the $\bar{\ell}=6$ mode. As can be seen from there, the coefficients only weakly depend on the index set $(\ell,\ell',\ell'')$, with $D^{6\ell\ell'\ell''}_{6\ell\ell'\ell''}\sim \mathcal{O}(0.1)$. It also follows form this, if $\alpha = 0$ two-mode initial data (as chosen in this work) is required to activate the non-trivial couplings induced by $D^{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''}_{\bar{\ell}\ell\ell'\ell''}$ between the $\ell=m$ modes\footnote{Single-mode initial data sources all odd-$\bar{\ell}=\bar{m}$ modes if $\alpha\neq 0$.}. If $\alpha=0$, but $\kappa>0$, initial data with support only over the $\ell=m=1$ mode leads to no mixing into other $\ell=m$ modes, and induces only a single mode-level self-interaction through $D^{1111}_{1111}=3/(10\pi)$; this results in a smooth (non-turbulent) saturation of the $\ell=m=1$ instability from such single-mode initial data.
